__d(function(e,t,n,a,i,s,o){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:4,chapter_title:"Simple Equations",estimated_study_time_minutes:55,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Understand what an equation is and how it differs from an expression","Identify the LHS (left hand side), RHS (right hand side), and the variable in an equation","Solve simple linear equations in one variable using the balancing method","Solve equations using the transposing method","Set up equations from word problems and solve them","Verify solutions by substituting back into the original equation","Understand that the same operation applied to both sides preserves equality"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Knowledge of variables and algebraic expressions from earlier classes","Understanding of the four operations on integers","Basic understanding of LHS and RHS in mathematical statements","Concept of equality \u2014 that both sides of '=' must represent the same value"],introduction:{why_important:"Equations are the language of mathematics applied to real-world problem solving. Almost every real-life problem \u2014 calculating age, distance, price, time \u2014 can be set up as an equation and solved systematically. Algebra, calculus, physics, engineering, economics \u2014 all rest on the foundation of equation-solving.",real_life_relevance:"If you earn \u20b9x per day and \u20b91,500 in 5 days, then 5x = 1500 \u2192 x = \u20b9300/day. If a rectangular field has perimeter 48 m and length is 3 more than width, you can set up and solve an equation to find the dimensions. Equations turn guesswork into certainty.",karnataka_context:"A Bengaluru IT professional calculates project timelines using equations. A Mysuru silk weaver calculates thread required using equations. A Karnataka farmer computes fertiliser quantity per acre using simple equations. Even splitting a restaurant bill in Bengaluru uses equation thinking: 'if 4 people pay equal shares and one coupon reduces total by \u20b9200, what does each person pay?'",connection_to_previous_learning:"Class VI introduced expressions and variables. Class VII introduces the equation \u2014 an expression with an equality sign \u2014 and the systematic method for finding the value of the unknown variable that makes the equation true."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"Variable (x, y, n\u2026)",role:"The unknown quantity we are trying to find \u2014 represents a number we don't yet know"},{name:"Equation",role:"A mathematical statement that two expressions are equal \u2014 contains an '=' sign and a variable"},{name:"The Balance Method",role:"Treating the equation as a balance scale \u2014 whatever you do to one side, you must do to the other to keep it balanced"},{name:"Transposition",role:"Moving a term from one side of the equation to the other, changing its sign \u2014 a shortcut for the balance method"}],plot:["The chapter opens with a puzzle: 'I think of a number, add 5 to it, and get 12. What is the number?' This sets up: x + 5 = 12.","It introduces the concept of an equation \u2014 a statement asserting equality between two expressions \u2014 and defines LHS, RHS, and variable.","It develops the balancing method: an equation is like a balance scale; performing the same operation on both sides maintains equality.","It shows how to solve equations: x + 5 = 12 \u2192 subtract 5 from both sides \u2192 x = 7.","It introduces the transposing method as a shortcut: moving a term to the other side changes its sign.","It solves progressively complex equations: linear equations with variables on one side, then with variables and constants on both sides.","It develops word problems \u2014 translating English sentences into equations and solving them.","The chapter closes by emphasising verification \u2014 always substitute the answer back into the original equation to confirm."],moral:"An equation is a puzzle with a unique solution. The balancing method provides a systematic, reliable way to solve any linear equation. Checking your answer by substitution is not optional \u2014 it is the mathematical proof that your answer is correct."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"What is an Equation?",simple_explanation:"An equation is a mathematical statement that two expressions are equal, connected by an '=' sign. It contains at least one variable (unknown). An expression does NOT have an '=' sign; an equation DOES.",detailed_explanation:"Expression vs Equation: '3x + 5' is an EXPRESSION (no equals sign). '3x + 5 = 14' is an EQUATION (has equals sign). Components: LHS (Left Hand Side) = the expression to the left of '='. RHS (Right Hand Side) = the expression to the right. Variable = the unknown (x, y, n, etc.). Solution = the value of the variable that makes LHS = RHS. Example: In 2x \u2212 3 = 7: LHS = 2x\u22123, RHS = 7, Variable = x. Solution: 2(5)\u22123 = 7 \u2713 \u2192 x=5 is the solution. An equation is like a balance scale \u2014 both sides weigh the same. Disturbing one side without doing the same to the other breaks the balance.",real_life_example:"'I think of a number, double it, and subtract 4 to get 10.' Equation: 2x \u2212 4 = 10. Here x is the mystery number. Solving: 2x = 14, x = 7. Check: 2(7)\u22124 = 14\u22124 = 10 \u2713.",karnataka_example:"'A Bengaluru auto driver charges \u20b915 for the first km and \u20b911 per km after that. A passenger pays \u20b948. How many km did they travel after the first km?' Equation: 15 + 11n = 48 \u2192 11n = 33 \u2192 n = 3 km.",common_mistakes:"Students confuse expressions with equations. '2x+3' is NOT an equation \u2014 you cannot 'solve' it because there is no equals sign. Only equations can be solved. Also, students sometimes think an equation can have any number of solutions \u2014 a simple linear equation in one variable has exactly ONE solution.",memory_tip:"Equation = EQUAL (has = sign). Expression = just a phrase (no = sign). 'Equation has an EQUAL sign. Expression is just an expression \u2014 it expresses a value but makes no claim about what it equals.' E = equation = equal sign present."},{concept_name:"Solving Equations \u2014 Balancing and Transposing",simple_explanation:"Balancing: perform the same operation on both sides to isolate the variable. Transposing: move a term to the other side, changing its sign. Both methods give the same result.",detailed_explanation:"Balancing Method: Goal = get the variable alone on one side. Rule = whatever you do to one side, do exactly the same to the other. Examples: x + 8 = 15 \u2192 subtract 8 from both sides \u2192 x = 7. 3x = 21 \u2192 divide both sides by 3 \u2192 x = 7. 2x \u2212 5 = 11 \u2192 add 5 to both sides \u2192 2x = 16 \u2192 divide by 2 \u2192 x = 8. Transposing Method (shortcut): when a term moves to the other side, its sign changes. Addition becomes subtraction; subtraction becomes addition; multiplication becomes division; division becomes multiplication. Example: x + 8 = 15 \u2192 x = 15 \u2212 8 = 7 (8 'transposed' to RHS, sign changed from + to \u2212). 3x = 21 \u2192 x = 21/3 = 7 (3 'transposed', \xd7 becomes \xf7). Complex: 2x + 3 = x + 7 \u2192 2x \u2212 x = 7 \u2212 3 \u2192 x = 4.",real_life_example:"A shopkeeper has some apples (x). After selling 23 and buying 15 more, he has 42. Equation: x \u2212 23 + 15 = 42 \u2192 x \u2212 8 = 42 \u2192 x = 50. He started with 50 apples. Verify: 50 \u2212 23 + 15 = 27 + 15 = 42 \u2713.",karnataka_example:"Ravi and his sister together have \u20b9240. Ravi has \u20b930 more than his sister. Find each person's amount. Let sister's amount = x. Ravi's = x+30. Equation: x + (x+30) = 240 \u2192 2x + 30 = 240 \u2192 2x = 210 \u2192 x = 105. Sister: \u20b9105, Ravi: \u20b9135. Check: 105+135 = 240 \u2713.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes transpose incorrectly \u2014 forgetting to change the sign. If 'x + 5 = 12' and they write 'x = 12 + 5 = 17' \u2014 that is WRONG (5 should become \u22125 on the other side). Always: when you MOVE a term across the '=' sign, FLIP its sign. Also, students forget to verify \u2014 always substitute the answer back to check.",memory_tip:"TRANSPOSE = MOVE and CHANGE SIGN. + becomes \u2212, \u2212 becomes +, \xd7 becomes \xf7, \xf7 becomes \xd7. Think of 'teleporting' a term \u2014 it arrives on the other side in opposite form. Verify ALWAYS: substitute x back and check LHS = RHS."},{concept_name:"Setting Up Equations from Word Problems",simple_explanation:"Translate English statements into mathematical equations by identifying the unknown (variable), the relationship between quantities, and expressing that relationship as an equation. Then solve and verify.",detailed_explanation:"Steps for word problems: (1) READ carefully \u2014 identify what is unknown. (2) ASSIGN \u2014 let the unknown = x (or any variable). (3) TRANSLATE \u2014 convert the English conditions into an equation using mathematical operations. (4) SOLVE \u2014 use balancing or transposing. (5) VERIFY \u2014 substitute back and check. (6) STATE THE ANSWER \u2014 answer the question asked, with units. Key translation phrases: 'sum' = addition, 'difference' = subtraction, 'product' = multiplication, 'quotient' = division, 'exceeds by' = adds to, 'twice' = 2\xd7, 'thrice' = 3\xd7, 'is' or 'equals' = '='.",real_life_example:"The sum of three consecutive integers is 72. Let the integers be n, n+1, n+2. Equation: n + (n+1) + (n+2) = 72 \u2192 3n + 3 = 72 \u2192 3n = 69 \u2192 n = 23. Integers: 23, 24, 25. Check: 23+24+25 = 72 \u2713.",karnataka_example:"A Karnataka farmer has a rectangular field. Its length is twice its breadth. Its perimeter is 120 m. Find the dimensions. Let breadth = x. Length = 2x. Perimeter = 2(l+b) = 2(2x+x) = 6x = 120 \u2192 x = 20 m. Breadth = 20 m, Length = 40 m. Check: 2(40+20) = 2\xd760 = 120 m \u2713.",common_mistakes:"Students struggle with 'consecutive integers' \u2014 remember they are n, n+1, n+2 (not n, 2n, 3n). Also, 'a number is 5 more than another' means x+5 (not 5x). Carefully read: 'twice a number' = 2x, but 'a number twice' means the same thing. Always define what x represents before writing the equation.",memory_tip:"Word problem steps: READ \u2192 ASSIGN x \u2192 TRANSLATE to equation \u2192 SOLVE \u2192 VERIFY \u2192 ANSWER. 'RATS-VA': Read, Assign, Translate, Solve, Verify, Answer. Never skip the Verify step \u2014 it proves your answer is correct."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Equation",meaning:"A mathematical statement asserting that two expressions are equal, connected by an '=' sign. Contains at least one variable. Example: 3x + 2 = 11."},{term:"Expression",meaning:"A mathematical phrase consisting of numbers, variables, and operations \u2014 but WITHOUT an equals sign. Example: 3x + 2. Expressions cannot be 'solved'; equations can."},{term:"Variable",meaning:"A symbol (usually a letter like x, y, or n) that represents an unknown or changing quantity in an equation."},{term:"LHS (Left Hand Side)",meaning:"The expression to the left of the equals sign in an equation. In 2x + 3 = 11, the LHS is '2x + 3'."},{term:"RHS (Right Hand Side)",meaning:"The expression to the right of the equals sign in an equation. In 2x + 3 = 11, the RHS is '11'."},{term:"Solution",meaning:"The value of the variable that makes both sides of the equation equal. Also called the 'root' of the equation. Example: x = 4 is the solution of 2x + 3 = 11."},{term:"Balancing Method",meaning:"A method of solving equations by performing the same mathematical operation on both sides to maintain equality, ultimately isolating the variable."},{term:"Transposing",meaning:"Moving a term from one side of an equation to the other, changing its sign in the process. Addition becomes subtraction; multiplication becomes division."},{term:"Linear Equation",meaning:"An equation where the highest power of the variable is 1. Example: 3x + 2 = 11 is linear. 3x\xb2 + 2 = 11 is NOT linear (x has power 2)."},{term:"Verification",meaning:"The process of checking that the solution is correct by substituting the value of the variable back into the original equation and confirming LHS = RHS."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Solve by Balancing",question:"Solve: 4x \u2212 3 = 13",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Add 3 to both sides (to eliminate \u22123 from LHS).","4x \u2212 3 + 3 = 13 + 3 \u2192 4x = 16.","Step 2: Divide both sides by 4.","4x/4 = 16/4 \u2192 x = 4.","Verify: LHS = 4(4) \u2212 3 = 16 \u2212 3 = 13 = RHS \u2713.","Answer: x = 4."]},{example_number:2,type:"Solve by Transposing",question:"Solve: 2x + 7 = 3x \u2212 4",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Transpose 3x to LHS: 2x \u2212 3x + 7 = \u22124.","\u2212x + 7 = \u22124.","Step 2: Transpose 7 to RHS: \u2212x = \u22124 \u2212 7 = \u221211.","Step 3: x = 11 (multiply both sides by \u22121).","Verify: LHS = 2(11)+7 = 22+7 = 29. RHS = 3(11)\u22124 = 33\u22124 = 29. LHS=RHS \u2713.","Answer: x = 11."]},{example_number:3,type:"Word Problem",question:"The sum of two consecutive even numbers is 74. Find the numbers.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Let the first even number = x. Next consecutive even number = x + 2.","Step 2: Equation: x + (x+2) = 74 \u2192 2x + 2 = 74.","Step 3: Transpose 2: 2x = 72 \u2192 x = 36.","Step 4: Numbers are 36 and 38.","Verify: 36 + 38 = 74 \u2713."]},{example_number:4,type:"Word Problem",question:"Sachin's age is 3 times his son's age. 10 years later, Sachin will be twice his son's age. Find their present ages.",step_by_step_answer:["Let son's present age = x. Sachin's present age = 3x.","After 10 years: Son = x+10. Sachin = 3x+10.","Equation: 3x + 10 = 2(x + 10).","3x + 10 = 2x + 20.","Transpose: 3x \u2212 2x = 20 \u2212 10 \u2192 x = 10.","Son's age = 10 years. Sachin's age = 30 years.","Verify: After 10 years: Son=20, Sachin=40. Is 40 = 2\xd720? Yes \u2713."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Simple Equations \u2014 Balancing, Transposing, Word Problems",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What is the difference between an expression and an equation?",a:"An expression has no '=' sign (e.g., 3x+5). An equation has an '=' sign and states that two expressions are equal (e.g., 3x+5 = 14). Only equations can be solved."},{type:"Solve",q:"Solve: 5x + 2 = 17",a:"5x = 17\u22122 = 15. x = 15/5 = 3. Verify: 5(3)+2 = 15+2 = 17 \u2713."},{type:"Solve",q:"Solve: 3(x \u2212 2) = 12",a:"3x \u2212 6 = 12. 3x = 18. x = 6. Verify: 3(6\u22122) = 3\xd74 = 12 \u2713."},{type:"Application",q:"A number when multiplied by 4 and then reduced by 6 gives 18. Find the number.",a:"Equation: 4x \u2212 6 = 18. 4x = 24. x = 6."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why must you verify the solution of an equation?",a:"Verification confirms the solution is correct. If LHS \u2260 RHS after substituting, there is a calculation error. It is the mathematical proof of correctness."}]}],key_takeaways:["An equation has an '=' sign; an expression does not. Only equations can be solved.","LHS = left of '='; RHS = right of '='; variable = unknown to be found.","Balancing method: perform the same operation on both sides to isolate the variable.","Transposing: move a term to the other side and change its sign (+ \u2194 \u2212, \xd7 \u2194 \xf7).","Steps to solve: isolate the variable, simplify, find x.","Always verify: substitute the solution back into the original equation and check LHS = RHS.","Word problems: Read \u2192 Assign variable \u2192 Translate \u2192 Solve \u2192 Verify \u2192 State answer.","Consecutive integers: n, n+1, n+2. Consecutive even/odd: n, n+2, n+4.","'Twice a number' = 2x. 'Exceeds by 5' = x+5. 'Is' or 'equals' = '='.","A linear equation in one variable has exactly ONE solution."],mind_map:{root:"Simple Equations",branches:[{branch:"What is an Equation?",sub:["Has '=' sign (unlike expression)","LHS = RHS","Variable = unknown","Solution = value making LHS = RHS"]},{branch:"Solving Methods",sub:["Balancing: same op on both sides","Transposing: move term, flip sign","+ \u2194 \u2212, \xd7 \u2194 \xf7 when transposing","Goal: isolate variable on one side"]},{branch:"Verification",sub:["Substitute solution into original equation","Check LHS = RHS","If not equal: error in working","Always verify!"]},{branch:"Word Problems",sub:["Read \u2192 Assign x \u2192 Translate","Solve \u2192 Verify \u2192 State answer","Consecutive: n, n+1, n+2","Consecutive even/odd: n, n+2, n+4"]},{branch:"Key Translations",sub:["'sum' = +","'difference' = \u2212","'twice/thrice' = 2x/3x","'exceeds by' = +","'is/equals' = ="]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 4 \u2014 Simple Equations. Equation = mathematical statement with '=' sign containing a variable. Expression = no '=' sign. LHS = left of '='; RHS = right. Variable = unknown (x, y, n). Balancing method: same operation on both sides to isolate variable. Transposing: move term across '=', change sign (+ \u2192 \u2212, \xd7 \u2192 \xf7). Solving: add/subtract to remove constants, multiply/divide to remove coefficients. Verification: substitute answer back \u2014 LHS must equal RHS. Word problems: Assign variable \u2192 write equation \u2192 solve \u2192 verify \u2192 answer. Key phrases: sum(+), difference(\u2212), twice(2x), 'is'(=), 'exceeds by n'(+n). Consecutive integers: n, n+1, n+2. Consecutive evens/odds: n, n+2, n+4. Linear equation in one variable has exactly one solution.",must_remember_facts:["Equation = has '=' sign. Expression = no '=' sign. Only equations are solved.","Balancing: do the SAME operation to BOTH sides.","Transposing: term moves to other side \u2192 sign changes (+ \u2192 \u2212, \xd7 \u2192 \xf7).","To solve ax + b = c: transpose b \u2192 ax = c\u2212b; divide by a \u2192 x = (c\u2212b)/a.","Verification: substitute x back. If LHS = RHS \u2192 correct. If not \u2192 error.","Word problems: Read, Assign (let x = ?), Translate (write equation), Solve, Verify.","Consecutive integers: n, n+1, n+2. Their sum = 3n+3.","Consecutive even/odd integers: n, n+2, n+4. Their sum = 3n+6.","'Twice' = 2\xd7. 'Thrice' = 3\xd7. 'Exceeds by k' = +k. 'Is' = '='.","A simple linear equation in one variable has exactly ONE unique solution."]}},2588,[]);